In C, you can terminate a program by calling the exit function. The exit function takes an integer argument, which represents the exit status. The exit status is returned to the process that started the program.

Here's an example of how to use the exit function:
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main ( void ) {
  printf ( "<p>Hello, world!</p>" ) ;
  exit ( EXIT_SUCCESS ) ;
}
```
In the example above, the main function prints a message and then calls the exit function. The exit function terminates the program and returns the exit status, which is set to EXIT_SUCCESS.
